## 15. Strict JSON-Based AI Integration

Create a centralized AI service that communicates with the configured multimodal model.

The AI should return structured JSON that conforms to predefined Pydantic schemas.

For example, a safety analysis response should follow this structure:

{
"analysis_id": "generated-identifier",
"risk_level": "medium",
"summary": "Potential hazards were observed in the uploaded image.",
"hazards": [
{
"title": "Missing visible edge protection",
"severity": "high",
"description": "An exposed edge appears to lack visible protective barriers.",
"evidence": "The edge is visible in the uploaded image.",
"recommended_action": "Have a competent site supervisor inspect the area and implement appropriate fall protection."
}
],
"recommendations": [
"Restrict access to the potentially hazardous area until it has been assessed."
],
"limitations": [
"The assessment is based only on the supplied image."
]
}

Treat this JSON as a schema example rather than a predetermined result. Actual output must reflect the uploaded content.

Requirements:

* Validate every AI response against its schema.
* Handle malformed JSON.
* Handle missing fields.
* Handle model timeouts.
* Handle API rate limits.
* Prevent invalid responses from crashing the frontend.
* Never assume the model will always return valid JSON.
* Keep prompts, model settings, and provider configuration centralized.
* Use explicit response models for every major feature.

Do not fabricate successful AI results when the AI provider is unavailable.

